{"arm": "pinned:local/qwen3.8-27b", "requested_model": "local/qwen3.8-27b", "question_id": "8058", "dataset": "MMLU-Pro", "category": "math", "fold": "calibration", "started_at": 1787606248.8055081, "latency_ms": 3274.2095419962425, "http_status": 200, "attempts": 1, "error": null, "text": "To find the determinant of the matrix $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}$, we can use cofactor expansion along the first row.\n\nThe formula for the determinant using the first row elements $a_{11}, a_{12}, a_{13}$ is:\n$$ \\det(A) = a_{11}C_{11} + a_{12}C_{12} + a_{13}C_{13} $$\nwhere $C_{ij}$ is the cofactor corresponding to element $a_{ij}$.\n\n1.  **Calculate the term for $a_{11} = 0$:**\n    $$ a_{11}C_{11} = 0 \\cdot \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 8 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = 0 $$\n\n2.  **Calculate the term for $a_{12} = 1$:**\n    The cofactor $C_{12} = (-1)^{1+2} \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = -1 \\cdot (7 \\cdot 4 - 3 \\cdot 6)$\n    $$ \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = 28 - 18 = 10 $$\n    So, $C_{12} = -10$.\n    $$ a_{12}C_{12} = 1 \\cdot (-10) = -10 $$\n\n3.  **Calculate the term for $a_{13} = 2$:**\n    The cofactor $C_{13} = (-1)^{1+3} \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\end{pmatrix} = 1 \\cdot (7 \\cdot 5 - 8 \\cdot 6)$\n    $$ \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 7 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\end{pmatrix} = 35 - 48 = -13 $$\n    So,", "finish_reason": "length", "served_model": "Qwen/Qwen3.8-27B", "prompt_tokens": 160, "completion_tokens": 512, "total_tokens": 672, "reasoning_tokens": null, "headers": {"vsr_selected_model": "local/qwen3.8-27b", "vsr_response_path": "upstream"}, "extracted": "A", "correct": false}
{"arm": "pinned:sclv/gemma-4", "requested_model": "sclv/gemma-4", "question_id": "2926", "dataset": "MMLU-Pro", "category": "biology", "fold": "test", "started_at": 1787606250.7061481, "latency_ms": 1718.179000003147, "http_status": 200, "attempts": 1, "error": null, "text": "During normal daytime environmental conditions, plants open their stomata to allow carbon dioxide ($\\text{CO}_2$) to enter the leaf for photosynthesis. Simultaneously, water vapor ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$) exits the leaf through these same openings via a process called transpiration.\n\nDue to the steep concentration gradient between the saturated interior of the leaf and the typically drier outside air, water vapor diffuses out of the leaf much more rapidly than $\\text{CO}_2$ diffuses in. For every molecule of $\\text{CO}_2$ that enters the leaf, hundreds of molecules of $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$ are typically lost.\n\n- **$\\text{CO}_2$** moves **into** the leaf (for photosynthesis).\n- **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$** moves **out** of the leaf (transpiration).\n- The rate of water loss (exit) is significantly higher than the rate of carbon dioxide gain (entry).\n\nComparing this to the options:\nOption I states: \"$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$ diffuses out of the leaf faster than $\\text{CO}_2$ enters.\" This accurately describes the physiological process.\n\nAnswer: I", "finish_reason": "stop", "served_model": "gemma-4", "prompt_tokens": 295, "completion_tokens": 259, "total_tokens": 554, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "headers": {"vsr_selected_model": "sclv/gemma-4", "vsr_response_path": "upstream"}, "extracted": "I", "correct": true}
